# Protection flags for benchmark/corpus_heap_variants/ -- the glibc heap
# corruption (tcache/fastbin) variant family.
#
# HELD IDENTICAL ACROSS EVERY TARGET IN THIS FAMILY ON PURPOSE. The only thing
# that varies between targets is WHICH HEAP PRIMITIVE the source exposes; a
# protection difference would confound that measurement.
#
# -fstack-protector-all  canary=ON. Irrelevant to a heap primitive by itself,
#                        which is the point: it must not be what stops the
#                        exploit, and a family that left it off would not model
#                        the modern case.
# -pie -fPIE             PIE=ON. Makes a code-address leak MANDATORY: every
#                        target's win() address is only known after the family's
#                        leak channel (show(), which dumps a job record raw,
#                        including its handler pointer) has been read.
# -Wl,-z,relro,-z,now    RELRO=FULL. The GOT is read-only, so the answer the
#                        R1 corpus uses for this category
#                        (benchmark/corpus/12_heap_tcache_poison: poison a
#                        tcache fd to hand out free@got) is STRUCTURALLY
#                        unavailable here. The writable code pointers are the
#                        program's own: a job record's handler on the heap, and
#                        the hooks[] table in .bss.
# -O0                    the family's geometry IS the measurement: the handler
#                        field must stay at a fixed offset inside the record and
#                        the indirect call must stay an indirect call. At higher
#                        -O gcc is free to inline default_run() through the
#                        pointer store and the target silently stops being in
#                        this category.
#
# NX is ON (default, no -z execstack).
#
# BUILT AND TESTED AGAINST
# ------------------------
#   glibc   2.35   (Ubuntu GLIBC 2.35-0ubuntu3.15)   <-- `ldd --version`
#   gcc     11.4.0 (Ubuntu 11.4.0-1ubuntu1~22.04.3)
#   kernel  6.8.0 x86-64, Ubuntu 22.04
#
# This matters more here than for any other category in the benchmark: every
# number in this family depends on glibc-version-specific allocator behaviour --
# safe-linking pointer mangling and the aligned_OK() check on a tcache pop (both
# glibc >= 2.32), the tcache `key` double-free scan (>= 2.29), the 7-entry
# tcache bin (mp_.tcache_count == 7), the 0x410-chunk tcache ceiling, and the
# fastbin "stash the rest into tcache" path. On a glibc < 2.32 the mangling step
# must be dropped; on a glibc with tcache disabled the bins change entirely. A
# later reader comparing results MUST check their own `ldd --version` first.
-fstack-protector-all
-pie
-fPIE
-Wl,-z,relro,-z,now
-O0
